\section*{Introduction}
Reading comprehension (RC) has been a focal point in natural language processing (NLP) for over a decade, driven by the development of numerous datasets and the creation of models capable of answering questions based on provided passages. However, most existing RC datasets are confined to a multiple-choice format, compelling models to generate answers that mirror the options presented. This constraint hinders the development of more flexible and expressive models, as it does not reflect the diverse questions users may wish to ask. To address this limitation, we propose a framework for open-domain RC that requires models to provide free-text responses. This shift offers greater flexibility in how answers are formulated and allows for a broader exploration of the passage.

To facilitate research in this area, we have developed a framework for open-domain RC that evaluates models on their ability to generate free-text answers. Specifically, we created the Free-text Generation from Passage (FGP) dataset, which consists of free-text questions and their corresponding answers derived from passages. We also established a metric to assess the quality of free-text answers and conducted experiments to compare various models. Our findings indicate that current models fail to generate high-quality answers in this setting. Additionally, we introduced a novel task for generating multiple-choice questions to facilitate the study of free-text RC. We hope this initiative will motivate the RC community to prioritize free-text generation as a key goal for future RC systems.

