\textbf{Related Work}
Recent advancements in unsupervised learning have yielded numerous techniques, including Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Adversarial Autoencoders (AAEs), and Variational Autoencoders (VAEs). These methods have been widely applied to tasks such as image generation, classification, and anomaly detection. However, a key limitation is their reliance on labeled data, which restricts their utility in real-world scenarios where data is often unlabeled. To overcome this, unsupervised learning has emerged as a solution, with unsupervised anomaly detection being a prominent area of research.

\textbf{Unsupervised Anomaly Detection}
In UAD, the objective is to identify anomalies within a dataset without requiring labeled examples. This is achieved by training a model on normal data to learn a representation that distinguishes between normal and abnormal patterns. Subsequently, the trained model evaluates new data points, assigning higher anomaly scores to those that deviate from the learned normal distribution.

\textbf{Domain Adaptive Feature Extractor}
Domain adaptation involves transferring knowledge from a source domain to a target domain. In the context of UAD, this entails learning domain-invariant representations that capture essential features while minimizing domain-specific noise. This process enables the model to generalize effectively to unseen domains.

\textbf{Domain Discriminator}
A domain discriminator is a classifier designed to differentiate between source and target domains. By incorporating this component, our method can adjust to target domain characteristics, thereby improving overall performance.

